## 8. Debugging & Performance Tips

| Issue | How to Diagnose | Fix |
|-------|-----------------|-----|
| SQL not logged | Enable `hibernate.show_sql=true` and `hibernate.format_sql=true` | Use log4j/slf4j to view queries |
| N+1 selects | Use `join fetch`, `entity graph`, or `@BatchSize` | Reduce number of roundtrips |
| Stale data | Enable transaction isolation `READ_COMMITTED` or use `@Version` for optimistic locking | Prevent lost updates |
| Large batches | Set `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size` and `hibernate.order_inserts=true` | Faster bulk ops |
| Cache miss | Check `hibernate.statistics` flag | Inspect cache stats, tune TTL |
| Slow startup | Use `hibernate.hbm2ddl.auto=none` | Avoid schema validation on every run |

---

## Quick Reference Cheat Sheet

| Code snippet | Description |
|--------------|-------------|
| `@Entity` | Marks a POJO as a persistence entity. |
| `@Table(name="…")` | Maps to a specific table. |
| `@Id @GeneratedValue` | Primary key generation. |
| `@Column(nullable=false, unique=true)` | Column constraints. |
| `@OneToMany(mappedBy="…", fetch=FetchType.LAZY)` | Parent side of a one‑to‑many. |
| `@ManyToOne(fetch=FetchType.LAZY)` | Child side of a many‑to‑one. |
| `EntityManager em = emf.createEntityManager();` | Create an `EntityManager`. |
| `em.persist(entity);` | Insert a new record. |
| `em.merge(entity);` | Update an existing record. |
| `em.remove(entity);` | Delete a record. |
| `TypedQuery<T> q = em.createQuery("SELECT e FROM Entity e", Entity.class);` | JPQL query. |
| `CriteriaBuilder cb = em.getCriteriaBuilder();` | Start a type‑safe Criteria query. |
| `@Transactional` | Spring or Jakarta EE transaction boundary. |
| `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` | Pull in JPA + Hibernate automatically. |
| `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update` | Auto‑create/update schema. |

---

## Recap

1. **Add dependencies** – JPA API + Hibernate (core).  
2. **Configure persistence** – `persistence.xml` or Java config.  
3. **Map your domain** – `@Entity`, relationships, constraints.  
4. **Obtain `EntityManager`** – CDI/Spring injection or manual factory.  
5. **Perform CRUD** – `persist/merge/remove/find`.  
6. **Write queries** – JPQL or Criteria; use fetch strategies to avoid N+1.  
7. **Leverage Spring Boot** – drastically reduces boilerplate.  
8. **Fine‑tune performance** – batching, caching, fetch plans.  

With this foundation you can build robust, transaction‑safe Java applications that leverage the full power of JPA and Hibernate. Happy coding!
